  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1155 AM CDT Sat Oct 3 2026\n\nEarlier this morning an upper level trough was located over the\nNorthern Rockies. An upper level ridge was located west of this\nupper trough and extended from British Columbia into Nevada. A\ncold front extended from Nebraska into Wyoming. South of this\ncold front there was a trough of low pressure at the surface \nacross eastern Colorado as a cool surface ridge slowly moves \neast across central and eastern Kansas. \n\nThe Northern Rockies upper trough will cross the northern \nplains today and as it does the Nebraska cold front will drop \nsouth into southwest Kansas overnight. As this cold front \npasses a reinforcement of cooler air will briefly settle into \nthe area late this weekend along with some drier air. This \nreinforcement of cooler air will not be significant but what it \nwill do is keep temperatures during the day mainly in the 70s \nwith lows mainly in the 40s to lower 50s through the start of \nthe upcoming work week. \n\nAfter Monday the temperatures will trend upward as westerly \ndownslope flow improves and 850mb temperatures to warm into the \n20 to 25C range by late day Tuesday ahead of another weak front\nthat will briefly drop into southwest Kansas mid week. \nCurrently ensembles not showing much, if any, cooler air with \nthis next frontal system as it settles into southwest Kansas mid\nweek but it will keep temperatures near or slightly above the \nseasonal normal for early October. The better chance for some \nwarmer temperatures to return to southwest Kansas will be during\nthe last half of the work week given the developing downslope \nflow, warming 850mb temperatures and a deepening surface lee \ntrough over eastern Colorado. Falling pressures along the lee of\nthe Rockies late will also result in a tightening surface \npressure gradient across southwest Kansas which will allow gusty\nsouth winds to return along with afternoon highs in the mid \n80s.",
